# Plan 08-17 (D-19, T-08-39): a tracked snapshot file (extension ivsn) is a rights problem by name.
files-unlisted-binary-ivsn
# Plan 15-02 (TOOL-08, D-49, GUARDRAILS-v0.3 V6): the host shim is a build-only shared object, so a
# tracked shared library is a rights-bearing, executable artifact refused by name. Two controls plant
# one file each - a .so and a .dylib - and the files step must refuse each by name; the one extension
# list in tools/check/binary-ext.txt carries all four shared-object extensions, so a third and a fourth
# control would prove the same line of code again (the tools/bench precedent). No important-gates row is
# written for either: PKG-06 stays Pending until Phase 16, and important-gates.sh refuses a row naming a
# Pending requirement. A control needs no row to run - the runner finds it by directory and this line.
files-unlisted-binary-so
files-unlisted-binary-dylib
# Plan 18-02 (TOOL-02, TOOL-09, GUARDRAILS-v0.4 W3): the inspection instrument's output is an operator-local,
# rights-bearing artifact, so a tracked one is refused by name. The extension list in tools/check/binary-ext.txt
# carries ivst and the files step refuses it; this control plants one file and proves that refusal. No
# important-gates row is written for it here: the registry step refuses a row naming a Pending requirement, and
# TOOL-09's row lands in the commit that flips it.
files-unlisted-binary-ivst
# Plan 09-07 (EV-03 item 4): the two-root output anchoring rule pins manifest-format for an
# output path outside fixtures/<dir>/ and outside data/.
manifests-output-anchor
# Plan 13-01 (RTS-01, GUARDRAILS-v0.3 V1): the rights ledger's own control. One overlay plants a row
# whose disposition key is absent, a cleared row with no source, a well-formed row absent from the
# inventory, and an operator page that omits one row's item phrase, so all four refusals are
# observed in one run. FORBID names rights-disposition even though the short-circuit rule makes it
# unreachable on that overlay: a checker that did NOT short-circuit would print an extra finding
# and fail this control instead of silently widening the set.
rights-ledger
# Plan 13-03 (RTS-02, GUARDRAILS-v0.3 V2): the naming limb's own control. One overlay plants the toy
# ledger's refused spelling in core/src/ (pinned, so the finding must not appear) and in runner/ (no
# pin, so it must be refused), and replaces the pin list with that matching pair plus one pin that
# matches nothing, so the suppressed direction, the refused direction and the stale direction are all
# observed in one run. FORBID names the pinned finding, the grammar finding and a missing pin list: a
# rule that ignored the pin would print the pinned finding, and a rule that silently dropped a bad
# line would print neither of the other two.
rights-naming
